// Cancellation (Review Focus 4): client disconnect → server write fails
// → pump closes the channel → producer send throws ChannelClosedException
// and the handler stops. D19: exhausted reader returns the connection to
// the pool (maxConnections=1 proves reuse); unexhausted close kills it.
import io;
import http;
import thread;
import concurrent;

// A file-scope variable cannot sit next to main(), so the producer's
// cancellation counter lives in a static field of a holder class.
class CancelCounters {
    public static AtomicInt stopped = AtomicInt(0);
}

// Producer body as a bound-method class (no anonymous function literals;
// see Global Constraints).
//
// The producer is paced (sleep between events) for the same reason a real SSE
// feed is: an unpaced producer pushes all its events through a capacity-1
// channel in milliseconds, faster than the client's disconnect can reach the
// server, so the pump would never hit a write error and there would be nothing
// to cancel — the vector would then pass or fail on a race instead of on D18.
class SseProducer {
    // A few tens of events is enough: the client disconnects after two, so the
    // rest is the window in which the cancellation has to show up. The count
    // stays small because thread.sleep() rounds up to the platform timer
    // resolution (~15 ms), which is what paces the feed.
    private static const int EVENTS = 60;
    private static const int EVENT_PACE_MS = 3;

    Channel<byte[]> m_ch;

    public SseProducer(Channel<byte[]> ch) { m_ch = ch; }

    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i < EVENTS; i = i + 1) {
            try {
                m_ch.send(io.toBytes("data: " + i.toString() + "\n\n"));
            } catch (ChannelClosedException e) {
                CancelCounters.stopped.incrementAndGet();
                return;
            }
            thread.sleep(EVENT_PACE_MS);
        }
        m_ch.close();
    }
}

HttpResponse sseHandler(HttpRequest req) {
    HttpResponse res = HttpResponse();
    res.bodyChannel = Channel(1);
    SseProducer p = SseProducer(res.bodyChannel);
    thread.spawn(p.run);
    return res;
}

int main() {
    HttpServer srv = HttpServer();
    srv.maxConnections = 1;
    srv.start("127.0.0.1", 0, sseHandler);
    string base = "http://127.0.0.1:" + srv.localPort().toString();
    HttpClient cli = HttpClient();

    // 1. Early disconnect: read 2 chunks, close → producer stops.
    HttpRequest r1 = HttpRequest();
    r1.url = Url.parse(base + "/sse");
    HttpResponse res1 = cli.open(r1);
    byte[] buf = byte[4096];
    int seen = 0;
    while (seen < 2) {
        int n = res1.bodyReader.read(buf, 5000);
        if (n < 0) { return 1; }
        seen = seen + 1;
    }
    res1.bodyReader.close();          // not exhausted → connection closed
    int waited = 0;
    while (CancelCounters.stopped.load() == 0 && waited < 5000) {
        thread.sleep(10);
        waited = waited + 10;
    }
    if (CancelCounters.stopped.load() == 0) { return 2; }

    // 2. Pool discipline: exhaust fully → connection returns to the
    // pool; maxConnections=1 means the next request must reuse it.
    HttpRequest r2 = HttpRequest();
    r2.url = Url.parse(base + "/sse");
    HttpResponse res2 = cli.open(r2);
    while (res2.bodyReader.read(buf, 5000) >= 0) { }
    res2.bodyReader.close();          // exhausted → back to pool
    HttpResponse res3 = cli.request(makeGet(base + "/sse"));   // buffered drain
    if (res3.statusCode != 200) { return 3; }

    cli.close();
    srv.close();
    io.print("ok");
    return 0;
}

HttpRequest makeGet(string url) {
    HttpRequest req = HttpRequest();
    req.url = Url.parse(url);
    return req;
}
